  {
    "episode_id": 78386,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78386.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Governor Mike Braun is here. So half time right now of the Indiana General Assembly. There've been some good news. But I think when it comes to the big story, it's SB1, which layman's terms is the property tax situation in the state of Indiana. It's SB1 for a reason. This was the signature issue for you, your administration. And I think for voters in the state of Indiana, We were talking off air. We do a little bit every time I'm here at the top of the four o'clock hour where I rattle off names of people who have no intentions of helping people like Nigel and myself here. What is your take on what you've seen so far with the property tax situation?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [],
    "assumptions": [],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 78386,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78386.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "So we won the battle in this game by being SB1. And when they go through it, they preserve the transparency part of it. Don't do referenda in off-year elections. That's important. They're interested in capping future increases. That's important. the part where they did the minimum amount in an accident that they're helping young entry-level homeowners and elderly retired. Well, we'd all be for that and they're going to see some real relief, but not to where it's going to do more than taking it back to where there wouldn't be a cut and they'd be most protected coming out of it. So there are a lot of taxpayers in the middle. And here's where it'll all be kind of worked out. I made it clear, honest, increase in costs, inflation, and Biden gave us. That was around 20, 25%, reasonable growth rate. So you can grow your local governments and school boards. Just don't remember the economy knocks it out of the park by growing 3% a year. Very seldom does it get there. So this is going to come down to when we have to point out how much their cash balances are. meaning that they had some windfall throughout that whole opaque process in what shiny objects have you already bought. And most of the bills went up more than 20 to 30%. Many went up 50% to doubled. So those were school districts and local governments that captured a windfall. And we'll get to a point where we start really fleshing that out. And we're going to win that argument. If not, it'll be clear to the taxpayers that they were not giving real relief, and that's the second half of the ball game.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
